  "slug": "power-of-two",
  "title": "Power of Two",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Bit manipulation",
  "patterns": [
    "bit manipulation"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "what does the binary representation of a power of two always look like? (1000...0, exactly one 1 bit)",
    "you already know n & (n - 1) clears the lowest set bit - what happens when you apply that to a number with only one set bit?",
    "if n has exactly one set bit, n & (n - 1) becomes 0; if n has more than one set bit, it doesn't",
    "check n > 0 first (0 and negatives are never powers of two), then check (n & (n - 1)) == 0"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting to exclude n <= 0 before the bit trick, since n & (n - 1) == 0 is also true for n = 0",
    "using a loop-based division check when the O(1) bit trick is cleaner and less error-prone",
    "treating negative n as valid just because the bit pattern happens to satisfy some check"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an integer `n`.\n\n**Return true if `n` is a power of two (that is, `n` can be written as 2^k for some non-negative integer k), and false otherwise.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "-2^31 <= n <= 2^31 - 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n = 0 (not a power of two)",
      "n = 1 (2^0, is a power of two)",
      "n is negative (never a power of two)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
